---
import ServiceLayout from '../../layouts/ServiceLayout.astro';
import MediaSection from '../../components/MediaSection.astro';
import PhotoPair from '../../components/PhotoPair.astro';
import { industries } from '../../data/industries';
import hero from '../../assets/industries/data-center/smart-cooler-breakroom.jpeg';
import campus from '../../assets/industries/data-center/dfw-office-campus.jpeg';
import lobbyVending from '../../assets/industries/data-center/lobby-smart-vending.jpg';
import coolerTap from '../../assets/industries/data-center/smart-cooler-tap.jpg';
import cardReader from '../../assets/industries/data-center/smart-cooler-card-reader.jpg';
import smartStore from '../../assets/industries/data-center/smart-store-drinks.jpg';

const faqs = [
	{
		question: 'Can you service a data center with strict access controls?',
		answer:
			'Yes. We plan every service visit around your badge rules and shift schedule. Equipment reports sales in real time, so visits are planned in advance and focused on what is running low.',
	},
	{
		question: 'Does a data center qualify for free placement?',
		answer:
			'A location qualifies with at least 50 people in total foot traffic, counting employees and visitors. At a data center, contractors and vendors on site count as visitors. Qualifying sites pay $0 for equipment, delivery, and setup.',
	},
	{
		question: 'How do overnight technicians buy food?',
		answer:
			'Smart coolers unlock with a tap and charge automatically for what is taken, and micro markets are self-checkout and open around the clock. No cashier and no cash are needed.',
	},
	{
		question: 'What equipment fits a small data center break room?',
		answer:
			'An AI smart cooler holds fresh meals, snacks, and drinks in a small footprint. Larger sites with more staff may add vending machines or a micro market.',
	},
	{
		question: 'What happens if equipment fails overnight?',
		answer:
			'Local technicians respond to reported issues within 24 hours, including after hours.',
	},
];
---

<ServiceLayout
	title="Data Center Vending Services in DFW"
	description="Free smart coolers, micro markets, and vending for Dallas–Fort Worth data centers. Self-service 24/7, cashless, with visits planned around your access rules."
	h1="Data Center Vending Services in DFW"
	eyebrow="Data Centers"
	intro="Texas Vending places AI smart coolers, micro markets, and vending machines in Dallas–Fort Worth data centers, so technicians and security staff can eat without leaving a secure site. Placement is free at qualifying locations, and every visit follows your access rules."
	heroImage={hero}
	heroAlt="Two-door smart cooler of drinks beside microwaves in a modern break room"
	serviceType="Data center vending services"
	crumb="Data Centers"
	parent={{ name: 'Industries We Serve', url: '/industries-we-serve/' }}
	related={{ heading: 'Other Industries We Serve', items: industries }}
	faqs={faqs}
	cta={{
		heading: 'Get Free Vending for Your Data Center',
		text: 'Share your staffing, shifts, and access requirements. We will walk the site with your team and recommend equipment. No cost and no long-term contract.',
	}}
>
	<MediaSection image={campus} alt="Glass office buildings and a full parking lot on a suburban business campus" heading="Secure Sites With Few Places to Eat" eyebrow="The challenge">
		<p>
			Data centers run all day and all night. Facility technicians, network engineers, and security officers work long
			shifts, and someone is always on call. Leaving the building means badging out, passing security, and badging back
			in. On a busy night, that is not an option.
		</p>
		<p>
			Many sites sit in business parks or on the edge of town, with little open nearby after dark. Contractors on a
			maintenance window or a build-out may be on site for long stretches with nowhere to go. The break room is often the
			only food on the property.
		</p>
		<p>
			During an outage or a major cutover, nobody leaves. Teams stay until the work is done, sometimes well past a normal
			shift. Food in the building keeps them going.
		</p>
		<p>
			Staff counts at a data center can be small compared with the floor space. Contractors, vendors, and visitors count
			toward the traffic that qualifies a site, so ask us even if your own team is lean.
		</p>
	</MediaSection>

	<MediaSection image={lobbyVending} alt="Two glass-door smart vending coolers with snacks and drinks in a marble lobby" heading="Compact, Self-Service Equipment" eyebrow="What we install" reverse>
		<p>Data center break rooms are often small. We recommend equipment that does a lot in little space:</p>
		<ul>
			<li><a href="/our-services/ai-smart-cooler/">AI smart coolers</a> for meals, snacks, and drinks behind one locked door</li>
			<li><a href="/our-services/vending-machine/">Vending machines</a> for snacks and energy drinks in security or NOC areas</li>
			<li><a href="/our-services/micro-markets/">Micro markets</a> for larger campuses with more staff</li>
			<li>Coffee machines for long overnight shifts; installation is free, and coffee and supplies are paid</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			A cooler stocked from our <a href="/fresh-food/">fresh food menu</a> gives the overnight crew sandwiches, wraps,
			salads, ready-to-eat meals, and protein snacks, not just chips. Drinks include Coca-Cola, Pepsi, Dr Pepper, Monster,
			and Red Bull.
		</p>
		<h3>Placement Inside a Secure Building</h3>
		<p>
			We look at where staff take breaks and which areas outside vendors can reach. A break room near the security desk,
			rather than inside a restricted zone, often makes restocking simpler for everyone. Your facilities and security
			teams decide what works, and we plan around their rules.
		</p>
	</MediaSection>

	<PhotoPair
		photos={[
			{
				image: coolerTap,
				alt: 'Hand opening a smart cooler door with protein shakes on the shelf',
				caption: 'One tap unlocks the door, and items put back before it closes are not charged.',
			},
			{
				image: cardReader,
				alt: 'Hand holding a credit card to the reader on a smart cooler',
				caption: 'Cards, mobile wallets, and tap-to-pay work at any hour.',
			},
		]}
	/>

	<MediaSection image={smartStore} alt="Woman taking a can from a smart cabinet stocked with drinks and toiletries" heading="Service Visits That Follow Your Access Rules" eyebrow="How we serve data centers">
		<ul>
			<li><strong>Planned around security.</strong> We schedule visits around your badge rules and shifts, and we set a restocking schedule for your site.</li>
			<li><strong>Fewer trips, better timed.</strong> Equipment reports sales in real time, so each visit brings what is running low.</li>
			<li><strong>No cashier needed.</strong> Smart coolers and micro markets are self-service and cashless.</li>
			<li><strong>24-hour response.</strong> Local technicians respond to reported issues within 24 hours, including after hours.</li>
		</ul>
		<p>
			Because restocking follows real sales data, we do not run a fixed route through your building. That means fewer
			visitors for your security team to log and badge in. Subsidized vending is also available on request if you want
			to offer food as a benefit for overnight crews.
		</p>
		<p>
			Have older machines on site? We provide <a href="/our-services/vending-repair/">vending machine repair</a> no matter who supplied them. To plan a site walk with your facilities and security teams, <a href="/contact-us/">contact Texas Vending</a>.
		</p>
	</MediaSection>
</ServiceLayout>
